# LOGIN(1)

## NAME

login - sign on

## SYNOPSIS

| **login** [ *username* ]

## DESCRIPTION

The *login* command is used when a user initially signs on. It is normally reached by getty(8),
which reads the name and passes it as the argument above.

If *login* is invoked without an argument, it asks for a user name, and, if appropriate, a
password. Echoing is turned off during the typing of the password, so it will not appear on the
written record of the session. A name that is not in the password file is still asked for a
password and still refused: this is deliberate, since a *login* that skipped the prompt would
say which names exist.

After a successful login, accounting files are updated and the user is informed of the
existence of mail and message-of-the-day files. *Login* initializes the user and group IDs and
the working directory, then executes a command interpreter (usually sh(1)) according to
specifications found in a password file. Argument 0 of the command interpreter is `-sh`.

## THIS MACHINE

**Note:** three of the files below are not on this system, and each fails quietly rather than
being cut out of the program:

/usr/adm/wtmp
: The permanent login history. Nothing reads it - there is no *last* here - so it is not on the
  image and the append is skipped.

/usr/spool/mail
: There is no mail(1), so `You have mail.` never prints.

/usr/bin
: Named by the PATH *login* exports, and empty of meaning: the shell's own default already
  reaches /bin.

**Note:** v7 says *login* `may be used at any time to change from one user to another`. Not
here. That use needs the program to be set-user-ID root, and it is not - getty(8) is already
root and hands the privilege on. The command that changes user is su(1), which is not yet
ported.

**Note:** v7 says *login* \`is recognized by sh(1) and executed directly (without forking)'.
That was the pre-Bourne shell. Sh(1) here has no such built-in and forks like any other
command.

## FILES

/etc/utmp
: accounting

/usr/adm/wtmp
: accounting; absent here

/usr/spool/mail/\*
: mail; absent here

/etc/motd
: message-of-the-day

/etc/passwd
: password file

## SEE ALSO

init(8), newgrp(1), getty(8), mail(1), passwd(1), passwd(5), ttys(5)

## DIAGNOSTICS

| `Login incorrect,` if the name or the password is bad.
| `No directory`, if the account's home directory cannot be reached; `No shell`, if the command interpreter cannot be executed.
